      "program_name": "Affordable Housing Density Bonus (Zoning Ordinance Chapter 130.31) and the Affordable Housing Ordinance in development (inclusionary with in-lieu fee)",
      "administering_agency": "El Dorado County Planning and Building Department, Planning Division (530-621-5355), with the Affordable Housing Task Force (established April 2, 2024, Resolution 046-2024)",
      "funding_type": "density bonus",
      "typical_award_size": "State Density Bonus Law (Government Code 65915) as implemented in Chapter 130.31: up to 50% density bonus for the standard affordability tiers, up to 80% or unlimited density for 100% affordable projects, plus concessions, waivers and reduced parking. No local bonus beyond state law was found.",
      "per_unit_cap": null,
      "affordability_requirement": "State tiers: 5% very low, 10% low, 10% moderate for-sale, or 100% affordable. The draft Affordable Housing Ordinance presented to the Task Force October 14, 2026 would require, for West Slope residential projects of 5 or more units or lots, 10% of rental units at 80% AMI for 55 years or 10% of for-sale units at 120% AMI for 45 years, with alternatives (in-lieu fee, off-site units, land dedication, conversion of market-rate units) and exemptions including deed-restricted affordable projects, mixed-use projects that are two-thirds or more residential, shelters and transitional housing, and projects where 51% or more of units are 1,600 square feet or less.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "new construction",
        "mixed-use",
        "homeownership"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "Density bonus is by right with the planning application. Resolution of Intention 163-2025 (November 4, 2025) started the Chapter 130.31 amendments; the Task Force's fee nexus study consultant was being selected in October 2026 and the ordinance and in-lieu fee will return to the Board after the study. No adoption date set.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "false",
      "layering_notes": "Entitlement tools, not money. Diamond Village II (31 units) was approved under SB 35 by the Planning Commission in January 2025, which shows the ministerial path works in the unincorporated county. Watch the inclusionary ordinance: a 100% affordable project is exempt, but mixed-income workforce projects on the West Slope would pick up a 10% at 80% AMI obligation or an in-lieu fee once adopted, and the Task Force is also studying Community Facilities Districts and Enhanced Infrastructure Financing Districts as infrastructure tools. Staff flagged litigation risk around inclusionary exactions. The Tahoe Basin portion of the county is also subject to TRPA allocations and coverage rules, which this ordinance would not cover (West Slope only).",
      "source_url": "https://eldorado.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=39161&GUID=DFA60236-9A16-41B1-9531-7361F8A62AB9&Options=&Search=&FullText=1",
